DTSTAMP:20261123T235057Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261123
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor proposes to update fid
 uciary rule to affirm Employee Retirement Income Security Act’s exclusiv
 e purpose rule
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor’s Employee Benef
 its Security Administration today issued proposed amendments to the invest
 ment duties regulation to clarify the fiduciary duty of loyalty under the 
 Employee Retirement Income Security Act.The proposed rule seeks to end the
  32-year uncertainty surrounding whether ERISA allows fiduciaries to consi
 der non-financial factors in retirement plan investments\, including envir
 onmental\, social\, and governance\, and diversity\, equity\, and inclusio
 n. The proposal gives fiduciaries an affirmative expression of their duty 
 of loyalty\, which would clarify that ERISA requires plan fiduciaries to a
 ct solely in the interest of the plan’s participants and beneficiaries\,
  and for the exclusive purpose of providing for the interests of participa
 nts and beneficiaries in their retirement income or financial benefits und
 er the plan.“President Trump’s Department of Labor is not putting its 
 thumb on the scale for or against any particular investment or asset class
 \,” said U.S. Secretary of Labor Keith Sonderling. “Our proposed rule 
 makes clear that retirement plan fiduciaries must make investment decision
 s solely in the financial interests of workers and retirees\, not to advan
 ce political or social agendas. Our focus is on protecting and growing Ame
 ricans’ retirement savings\, which have reached record levels thanks to 
 President Trump.”If finalized\, the proposal would also place a heighten
 ed focus on shareholder engagement and proxy voting by fiduciaries to secu
 re the retirement interests of American workers and would avoid the improp
 er pressures and influence placed on plan fiduciaries. This includes votin
 g shares and otherwise attempting to influence corporate behavior through 
 shareholder engagement activities for ESG/DEI purposes. The proposed rule 
 follows the directive in President Trump’s executive order\, “Protecti
 ng American Investors from Foreign-Owned and Politically Motivated Proxy A
 dvisors\,” which instructed the department to stre\nhttp://www.dol.gov/n
 ewsroom/releases/ebsa/ebsa20261123
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/ebsa/ebsa20261123
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:43035fbd6a725408@role-environmental-land-use.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261123T172614Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261116
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor cites powder coating c
 ompany for willful toxic substance exposures\, repeat violations\, propose
 s more than $1.6M in penalties
DESCRIPTION:HOUSTON – The U.S. Department of Labor has cited a powder 
 coating and furniture restoration company for willfully and repeatedly ex
 posing workers to toxic substances and other safety and health hazards.The
  department’s Occupational Safety and Health Administration conducted th
 ree inspections of Outdoor Furniture Refinishing Inc. – doing business a
 s Allied Powder Coating – on April 10\, 2026. One inspection addressed c
 omplaint items after the employer provided an inadequate response to an in
 quiry\, and two follow-up inspections were initiated in relation to safety
  and health violations found during a December 2023 inspection. The employ
 er’s failure to submit abatement documentation prompted a comprehensive 
 review of ongoing hazards.OSHA cited the company for three willful violati
 ons for exposing workers to hazardous levels of 1\,3\,5-triglycidyl isocya
 nurate and total dust during powder coating operations. The agency also is
 sued 18 serious violations for failing to provide training on excessive no
 ise exposure and for deficiencies involving respiratory protection\, hazar
 d communication\, personal protective equipment\, forklift operations\, el
 ectrical safety\, and portable ladders.In addition\, OSHA cited the compan
 y for 18 repeat violations related to respiratory protection\, noise overe
 xposure\, spray booths\, hazard communication\, compressed air\, and elect
 rical hazards. The agency also issued one other-than-serious violation for
  unsanitary working conditions.OSHA has proposed $1\,640\,888 in penalties
 .The agency found that\, despite multiple inspections since 2013 and a for
 mal settlement agreement finalized in December 2024\, the company has fail
 ed to take meaningful action to correct recurring violations.The employer 
 has 15 business days from receipt of its citations and penalties to comply
 \, request an informal conference with OSHA’s area director\, or contest
  the findings before the independent Occupational Safety and Health Review
  Commission. Check the OSHA establishment search p\nhttp://www.dol.gov/n
 ewsroom/releases/osha/osha20261116
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/osha/osha20261116
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:4c0e1a9bd92f2caa@role-environmental-land-use.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261123T172610Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261112
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Rangeland Management
DESCRIPTION:The U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA or Department) propos
 es to amend the regulations governing grazing and livestock use on the Nat
 ional Forest System to modernize grazing administration\, improve clarity\
 , codify existing policy\, and expand opportunities for stewardship and ac
 cess. The proposed amendments would update permit issuance\, eligibility\,
  and qualification provisions\; establish a framework for adaptive respons
 e to drought\, wildfire\, and similar disturbance events\; and expand auth
 orities for stewardship-oriented livestock use\, including adding the abil
 ity to implement stewardship projects (such as outcome-based grazing or ta
 rgeted grazing) under a temporary grazing permit or livestock use permit. 
 The proposed rule would revise grazing fee provisions to ensure consistent
  fee-setting across the national forests in the 16 contiguous Western Stat
 es and the national grasslands\, and establish a surcharge for authorized 
 leasing of livestock by permittees. It would consolidate and renumber exis
 ting sections in subpart A for clarity and make conforming\, technical\, a
 nd administrative updates throughout part 222. The Department invites publ
 ic comment on all aspects of the proposed rule.\nhttps://www.federalregist
 er.gov/documents/2026/11/12/2026-23033/rangeland-management
URL:https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/11/12/2026-23033/rangel
 and-management
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
